To explain briefly our reasoning:

• Field_3 has the highest threat (0.15) and already has two drones (Drone_1 and Drone_6) that are right where they need to be. We leave Field_3 as is.

• Two fields are in the next tier (threat 0.09): Field_2 and Field_4. Field_4 already has two drones protecting it but needs three for full protection; Field_2 currently has no protecting drones and requires four. Because our rule is to “fully protect the field with the highest threat level with the closest drones” and the available drones from the lowest‐priority field (Field_1, threat 0.06) are the only ones available to be reallocated, we check distances. The drones in Field_1 are relatively far from both Field_2 and Field_4, but it turns out that adding one extra drone (the one that is closest—in our case, Drone_3) to Field_4 will “complete” that field’s protection (3 drones needed, and now Field_4 gets three). 

• That leaves the remaining three drones from Field_1 (Drone_2, Drone_4, and Drone_8) to be sent toward Field_2. Although Field_2 requires four drones for full protection, we have only three available; in our resource‐limited situation the higher–threat fields are prioritized. So Field_2 ends up with three drones (partial protection) while Field_1 (the lowest threat field) loses its staff entirely.

Thus, the final assignment prioritizes complete protection of the two most-threatened fields (Field_3 at 0.15 and Field_4 at 0.09) while trying to cover Field_2 (also 0.09) as best as possible with our available drones.
